<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Progressive Party (The Gambia)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PPP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-progressive-party-guyana">People's Progressive Party</a> was a political party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a>, founded in 1959 and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawda-jawara">Dawda Jawara</a>. With strong support in the rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>, it won the elections of 1962, led the country to independence in 1965 and governed until the military coup of 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-progressive-party-the-gambia.jpg" alt="People's Progressive Party (The Gambia)" width="320" height="112" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: People's Progressive Party · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:People%27s_Progressive_Party_(Gambia)_logo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded as the Protectorate People's Party, a movement of the rural population against the hold of the urban areas on Gambian politics, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawda-jawara">Dawda Jawara</a> later changed its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-progressive-party-guyana">People's Progressive Party</a>. Jawara, elected to the legislature in 1960 and minister of education, led it from its founding until 1994. After its victory in the election of 1962 he became prime minister, and when the republican constitution came into force after the referendum of 1970 he became the first President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Discontent with the party's long rule gave rise to rival parties. Sheriff M. Dibba, the first vice-president, resigned after a political scandal in 1972 and founded the National Convention Party on 7 September 1975. In 1981 a civilian-led coup attempt with some support from the Gambia Field Force tried to overthrow the government; it was put down by Senegalese intervention, the dead are counted at between 500 and 1,000, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and the Gambia then formed the Senegambia Confederation. Because the coup was led by a former National Convention Party candidate, many of that party's members were arrested. Dibba still took 28 per cent of the presidential vote in 1987.</p><p>In 1994 officers of the Gambian National Army overthrew Jawara in a bloodless coup, accusing the government of corruption and a lack of economic development. The country was under military rule until 1996, when Yahya Jammeh was elected president for the new Alliance for Patriotic Reorientation and Construction, which won 33 of the 45 seats in the National Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The party was banned from elections under Jammeh, during whose rule there were numerous reports of human rights abuses and suppression of the media. Its leader Omar A. Jallow, a former minister, was reportedly jailed more than 22 times. The party joined the National Alliance for Democracy and Development in 2005 and Coalition 2016, whose candidate Adama Barrow won the presidential election of 2016. Jawara, exiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and later amnestied, resigned as leader in 2002 and died on 27 August 2019, aged 95.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Progressive Party (The Gambia)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constitutional reforms in the 1950s and early 1960s brought Dawda Jawara and his <b>People's Progressive Party</b> to power.</blockquote><a href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">Gambia Colony and Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
